WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
 

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.792
 

00:00:09.792 --> 00:00:11.500
DAVE WILLIAMS:
Understanding variation is

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.400
one of the key lenses of
our theory of improvement.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.030
As improvers, we aim to reduce
the variation of process

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:22.570
and create a process that achieves
a certain level of reliability.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:26.710
One tool that can help us
understand variation is a histogram.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.290
A histogram is a chart
that can help us understand

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:32.080
the distribution of continuous data.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.290
So for example, let's imagine you work
in a federally qualified health center,

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:40.300
and you are tasked to improve the
patient cycle time in a clinic.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.010
The patient's total time from
appointment start time to completion

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:47.250
is important to the patient experience
and important to the clinic's

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:48.440
efficiency.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.260
So using a data collection
form, you collected this data

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.030
for the cycle time of
patients in a single day.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.430
So imagine here we've got our minutes
going along the left hand column,

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:03.740
and we've got the number of patients
that had a cycle time within that time

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.830
interval on the right hand column.

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:08.375
And so what we're going to do
is draw the actual histogram.

00:01:08.375 --> 00:01:13.400
And a histogram looks very
much like a bar chart,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.330
but the bars are organized
by the time interval.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:20.770
So let's draw our bar chart.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.320
And again, we're working on cycle time.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.470
 

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:31.450
And along the x-axis we're
going to put in our times here.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:40.630
So we've got 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:43.800
And then I just lumped anything that's
over 60, because for the most part,

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.080
we don't want that.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.910
And then on this side, so this
is-- let me put my time here.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:52.820
It's good to label your axis.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.180
On this side, I'm going to
put my number of patients.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:03.020
So I'll go 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.825
And again, this is the y-axis.

00:02:04.825 --> 00:02:08.729
Now I want to label that as
well, so people who can see this

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:10.330
know what it is that we're tracking.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.070
So now I can come along
here with my data,

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.750
and we just create-- we go
with each time interval,

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:19.690
and we create a bar that corresponds
with the number of patients.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.360
So for patients that waited only
10 minutes, those lucky few,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.642
we're going to come along,
and we will do 2 patients.

00:02:28.642 --> 00:02:29.350
We'll make a bar.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:34.795
 

00:02:34.795 --> 00:02:38.650
Let's just color it in, so it's
a little bit easier to look at.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:39.750
Great.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.707
So now as you look at
your cycle time data,

00:02:41.707 --> 00:02:43.540
you can look for the
number of patients that

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.870
had cycle times in each of
the various time intervals.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.120
Once you've plugged in all the data,
you'll see the distribution of the data

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:54.400
and better understand the variation
that exists within your process.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.940
So two things to look for at the
start is, does this histogram

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.910
show a distribution that
appears symmetrical?

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:03.660
So if you look at this
chart, for example,

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.520
it looks kind of like a mountain.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.770
So it's pretty symmetrical.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:09.311
Now, it doesn't mean that you
like the variation that exists,

00:03:09.311 --> 00:03:12.480
and the times that are here, or that
you're happy with the amount of time.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.420
But this kind of shows that you've got a
process that doesn't have any outliers.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.710
And now you can work on trying to
figure out how to reduce the variation

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:23.100
and reduce the time to
whatever's appropriate for what

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.410
you're trying to improve.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:28.570
Now in some cases, you might encounter
an example where, for example,

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:33.360
here we came along, and let's imagine
this one's just off the charts.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.120
And it's actually 10, or
12, minutes, or even higher.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:43.520
And you notice that this particular
interval has an outlier or something

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:44.870
that just jumps out at you.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.440
And it says there's something
else going on here that's

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.380
an area that we need to figure out.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.000
And so that can be a
helpful thing to point out

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.092
when you're looking at
your histogram to identify

00:03:55.092 --> 00:03:58.050
if there are some special places that
may be in a certain time interval

00:03:58.050 --> 00:03:59.620
or after a certain point.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:04.140
There's something unusual that's
outside the symmetry of the process.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:17.995
 

